I'd recommend registering `PaymentService` as a **Riverpod provider** rather than with `get_it`. Your app already builds its whole dependency graph through Riverpod, and adding `get_it` for one service creates a second container with real costs:

- **Two lifecycles.** Riverpod disposes providers (e.g. `autoDispose`, scope teardown) while `get_it` singletons live until you manually reset them. A repository provider that reads a `get_it` service can outlive or underlive its dependency.
- **Tests stop working the usual way.** `ProviderScope(overrides: [...])` / `ProviderContainer(overrides: [...])` won't reach anything resolved through `get_it`, so payment tests need separate `GetIt.I.reset()` / re-registration setup.
- **Two places to look.** Anyone reading the code has to know which container constructs which object.

`get_it` is a good package, but switching DI containers should be a deliberate, app-wide migration, not a side effect of one feature. Here's the payments feature wired through Riverpod, following the existing pattern.

## Structure

```
lib/features/payments/
  data/
    payment_service.dart        # wraps the payment gateway / HTTP API
    payment_dto.dart            # gateway wire format
    payment_repository.dart     # maps DTOs → domain models
  domain/
    payment.dart                # domain model
  presentation/
    checkout_controller.dart    # UI state + commands
    checkout_screen.dart        # renders state, forwards intent
  payments_providers.dart       # the feature's DI wiring
```

## Service (external system boundary)

```dart
// data/payment_service.dart
class PaymentService {
  PaymentService(this._dio);
  final Dio _dio;

  Future<PaymentDto> createCharge({
    required int amountMinor,
    required String currency,
    required String paymentMethodId,
  }) async {
    final res = await _dio.post('/payments', data: {
      'amount': amountMinor,
      'currency': currency,
      'payment_method': paymentMethodId,
    });
    return PaymentDto.fromJson(res.data as Map<String, dynamic>);
  }
}
```

## Repository (maps to domain models)

The repository returns `Payment`, never `PaymentDto` or a `Dio` `Response`, so presentation code isn't tied to the gateway's format.

```dart
// domain/payment.dart
enum PaymentStatus { succeeded, pending, failed }

class Payment {
  const Payment({required this.id, required this.amountMinor,
      required this.currency, required this.status});
  final String id;
  final int amountMinor;
  final String currency;
  final PaymentStatus status;
}

// data/payment_repository.dart
abstract interface class PaymentRepository {
  Future<Payment> pay({required int amountMinor, required String currency,
      required String paymentMethodId});
}

class RemotePaymentRepository implements PaymentRepository {
  RemotePaymentRepository(this._service);
  final PaymentService _service;

  @override
  Future<Payment> pay({required int amountMinor, required String currency,
      required String paymentMethodId}) async {
    final dto = await _service.createCharge(
      amountMinor: amountMinor, currency: currency,
      paymentMethodId: paymentMethodId,
    );
    return Payment(
      id: dto.id,
      amountMinor: dto.amount,
      currency: dto.currency,
      status: switch (dto.status) {
        'succeeded' => PaymentStatus.succeeded,
        'processing' || 'requires_action' => PaymentStatus.pending,
        _ => PaymentStatus.failed,
      },
    );
  }
}
```

The interface is justified here because payments need a fake in tests. Real charges must never run in a test suite.

## Providers (feature DI wiring)

```dart
