## Client update: tolerate a missing `tier`

Model the field as optional at the parse boundary and keep `points` strict, since it was present in the spec and on the wire:

```dart
// lib/src/rewards/rewards_response.dart
enum RewardTier { bronze, silver, gold, platinum, unknown }

RewardTier _parseTier(Object? raw) {
  if (raw is! String) return RewardTier.unknown;
  return RewardTier.values.firstWhere(
    (t) => t.name == raw.toLowerCase(),
    orElse: () => RewardTier.unknown,
  );
}

class RewardsResponse {
  const RewardsResponse({required this.points, this.tier});

  final int points;

  /// Swagger marks this required, but staging omitted it (see contract note).
  /// Null means the server did not send a tier.
  final RewardTier? tier;

  factory RewardsResponse.fromJson(Map<String, dynamic> json) {
    final points = json['points'];
    if (points is! int) {
      throw FormatException('rewards.points missing or not an int', json);
    }
    return RewardsResponse(
      points: points,
      tier: json.containsKey('tier') ? _parseTier(json['tier']) : null,
    );
  }
}
```

If you're using `json_serializable` or `freezed`, the equivalent is to make `tier` nullable (`RewardTier? tier`) with `@JsonKey(unknownEnumValue: RewardTier.unknown)`. Don't regenerate from the Swagger as-is, or the strict `required` will come back.

UI and callers should handle `tier == null` explicitly, for example by hiding the tier badge or showing "Tier unavailable". Don't silently default to `bronze`, because that would show users wrong data.

Add a regression test that uses the real staging shape:

```dart
test('parses rewards response without tier (observed on staging)', () {
  final r = RewardsResponse.fromJson({'points': 120});
  expect(r.points, 120);
  expect(r.tier, isNull);
});

test('rejects response without points', () {
  expect(() => RewardsResponse.fromJson({'tier': 'gold'}),
      throwsFormatException);
});
```

## Contract status to record (PR description / changelog)

> **GET /rewards: contract NOT verified.** Swagger declares `points` and `tier` required. One authorized staging response (date, env, request ID: _fill in_) returned `points` and omitted `tier`. The client now treats `tier` as optional and surfaces its absence. Open question for the backend team: is `tier` conditionally omitted (e.g., unenrolled users, feature flag), or is this a server bug? Either the spec or the server needs to change.

