Use the window width to choose the navigation widget, and drive all three variants from **one destination list** and **one selected index**. That way the layouts can't drift apart, and switching sizes (rotation, window resizing, foldables) keeps the user's place.

## Breakpoints (Material 3 window size classes)

| Width | Navigation |
|---|---|
| < 600 | `NavigationBar` (bottom) |
| 600–839 | `NavigationRail` (collapsed, icons + optional labels) |
| ≥ 840 | `NavigationRail(extended: true)` or a persistent `NavigationDrawer` |

## Implementation

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class AppDestination {
  const AppDestination(this.label, this.icon, this.selectedIcon);
  final String label;
  final IconData icon;
  final IconData selectedIcon;
}

const destinations = <AppDestination>[
  AppDestination('Home', Icons.home_outlined, Icons.home),
  AppDestination('Search', Icons.search_outlined, Icons.search),
  AppDestination('Profile', Icons.person_outline, Icons.person),
];

class AdaptiveScaffold extends StatefulWidget {
  const AdaptiveScaffold({super.key, required this.pages});
  final List<Widget> pages;

  @override
  State<AdaptiveScaffold> createState() => _AdaptiveScaffoldState();
}

class _AdaptiveScaffoldState extends State<AdaptiveScaffold> {
  int _index = 0;

  void _select(int i) => setState(() => _index = i);

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    // Window width, not parent constraints: navigation is a window-level decision.
    final width = MediaQuery.sizeOf(context).width;

    // IndexedStack keeps each tab's state/scroll position alive across
    // tab switches AND across layout changes.
    final body = IndexedStack(index: _index, children: widget.pages);

    if (width < 600) {
      return Scaffold(
        body: body,
        bottomNavigationBar: NavigationBar(
          selectedIndex: _index,
          onDestinationSelected: _select,
          destinations: [
            for (final d in destinations)
              NavigationDestination(
                icon: Icon(d.icon),
                selectedIcon: Icon(d.selectedIcon),
                label: d.label,
              ),
          ],
        ),
      );
    }

    final Widget sideNav = width < 840
        ? NavigationRail(
            selectedIndex: _index,
            onDestinationSelected: _select,
            labelType: NavigationRailLabelType.all,
            destinations: [
              for (final d in destinations)
                NavigationRailDestination(
                  icon: Icon(d.icon),
                  selectedIcon: Icon(d.selectedIcon),
                  label: Text(d.label),
                ),
            ],
          )
        : NavigationDrawer(
            // Persistent drawer: placed inline, not in Scaffold.drawer.
            selectedIndex: _index,
            onDestinationSelected: _select,
            children: [
              const SizedBox(height: 16),
              for (final d in destinations)
                NavigationDrawerDestination(
                  icon: Icon(d.icon),
                  selectedIcon: Icon(d.selectedIcon),
                  label: Text(d.label),
                ),
            ],
          );

    return Scaffold(
      body: SafeArea(
        child: Row(
          children: [
            sideNav,
            const VerticalDivider(width: 1, thickness: 1),
            Expanded(child: body),
          ],
        ),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

If you'd rather keep a rail on wide screens instead of a drawer, replace the `NavigationDrawer` branch with the same `NavigationRail` and set `extended: true` and `labelType: NavigationRailLabelType.none`. Extended rails don't allow label types other than `none`.

## Things to get right

- **Persistent vs. modal drawer.** For a persistent drawer, put `NavigationDrawer` inline in the `Row`. Don't assign it to `Scaffold.drawer`, which makes it a modal overlay that the user has to open.
- **State preservation.** The selected index lives above the layout switch, so resizing doesn't reset the tab. `IndexedStack` keeps the pages alive. If you use **go_router**, use `StatefulShellRoute.indexedStack` and build this same switch inside its `builder`. Then `navigationShell.currentIndex` and `navigationShell.goBranch(i)` replace `_index` and `_select`, and deep links and per-tab back stacks work too.
- **Use `MediaQuery.sizeOf`, not `MediaQuery.of`.** It only rebuilds when the size changes, not on every inset or keyboard change.
- **Safe areas.** The bottom bar handles insets on its own. The `Row` layout needs `SafeArea` so the rail clears notches and camera cutouts in landscape.
- **Destination count.** A bottom bar works best with 3–5 items. If you have more, the rail and drawer can show them, but phones will need a "More" pattern.
- **Packages.** `flutter_adaptive_scaffold` has been discontinued, so don't adopt it for new code. The hand-rolled switch above is short and you control it fully.

## Testing

Write widget tests at each size class so a regression in one layout doesn't go unnoticed:

```dart
