## Fix: close the controller in `dispose()`

```dart
class SearchModel extends ChangeNotifier {
  final StreamController<String> _queries = StreamController<String>.broadcast();
  StreamSubscription<String>? _querySub;
  bool _disposed = false;

  Stream<String> get queries => _queries.stream; // read-only view

  SearchModel() {
    _querySub = _queries.stream.listen(_onQuery);
  }

  void submit(String q) {
    if (_disposed || _queries.isClosed) return; // no events after teardown
    _queries.add(q);
  }

  void _onQuery(String q) {
    // ...update state...
    if (!_disposed) notifyListeners();
  }

  @override
  void dispose() {
    _disposed = true;
    _querySub?.cancel();   // cancel subscriptions this class created
    _queries.close();      // close the controller it owns
    super.dispose();       // always last
  }
}
```

Key points:
- **The owner closes it.** The class that creates the `StreamController` closes it in `dispose()`. Don't count on garbage collection, because an open controller with active listeners stays reachable.
- **Cancel subscriptions too.** If the notifier subscribes to this controller or any other stream, keep the `StreamSubscription` and cancel it in `dispose()`.
- **Guard writes after disposal.** An async callback that finishes after `dispose()` would otherwise hit `add` on a closed controller, which throws `StateError`, or call `notifyListeners()` on a disposed notifier.
- **Expose only `stream`.** Don't expose the controller, so outside code can't add events or close it.

## Make sure `dispose()` actually runs

Closing in `dispose()` only helps if something calls `dispose()`:
- **Provider:** `ChangeNotifierProvider(create: (_) => SearchModel())` disposes the model for you. `ChangeNotifierProvider.value(...)` does **not**, so whoever created that instance has to dispose it.
- **StatefulWidget owner:** create the model in `initState` and call `_model.dispose()` in the widget's `dispose()`.
- Don't fix the leak by making the model a global or static singleton. That removes it from the widget lifecycle and hides the problem.

## Test the disposal path

```dart
