## Review: `softWrap: false` does not replace `maxLines`

The teammate's assumption is wrong. Removing `maxLines` breaks the one-line requirement.

**What each property controls:**

- **`softWrap`** controls *soft* line breaks only. These are the breaks the engine inserts at word boundaries when text reaches the available width. `softWrap: false` stops those.
- **Explicit newlines.** `softWrap: false` does **not** remove or ignore `\n` characters. A hard newline still starts a new line.
- **`maxLines`** caps the number of displayed lines, whatever caused the break. It is the only property here that enforces "exactly one line."

**Current behavior with `softWrap: false` and no `maxLines`:**

| Server copy | Result |
|---|---|
| `"Weekly summary"` | 1 line (works by accident) |
| `"Weekly\nsummary"` | **2 lines.** The compact title grows taller, or clips or overflows if the parent has a fixed height. |
| `"A very long title that exceeds the width"` | 1 line, but it runs past the box with no visible truncation. Overflow defaults to clip, so the copy is silently cut. |

The newline case is the regression. It only appears with real server data, which is why it probably passed a manual check.

## Proposed fix

```dart
Text(
  title,
  maxLines: 1,                       // enforces the one-line contract
  overflow: TextOverflow.ellipsis,   // visible truncation affordance
  softWrap: false,                   // optional; maxLines: 1 already limits output
)
```

Notes:

- **Restore `maxLines: 1`.** It is the contract. `softWrap` is optional once `maxLines` is set. Ellipsis still measures against the available width even with `softWrap: false`.
- **Choose the overflow mode deliberately.** `ellipsis` suits titles. Use `fade` only if design asks for it.
- **Decide how newlines should read, then make it explicit.** With `maxLines: 1`, `"Weekly\nsummary"` shows only `"Weekly"` plus truncation handling, and the rest is hidden. If product wants the full title flattened onto one line, normalize the copy before rendering:

  ```dart
  final displayTitle = title.replaceAll(RegExp(r'\s*\n\s*'), ' ').trim();
  ```

  That is a product decision. Keep `maxLines: 1` either way as the safety net.
- **Don't wrap the title in a fixed-height box** such as `SizedBox(height: 20)`. At large text scales a single line gets taller, and a fixed height will clip it. Use `minHeight` or padding instead.
- **If it sits in a `Row`** with an icon or action, wrap it in `Expanded` or `Flexible` so the ellipsis has a finite width to truncate against.

## Regression check

This test uses newline copy, long unbroken copy, and enlarged text scales. It asserts three things:

- exactly one rendered line (`didExceedMaxLines` plus a height comparison against a known one-line reference),
- no layout exception,
- truncation actually happens.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
import 'package:flutter/rendering.dart';
import 'package:flutter_test/flutter_test.dart';

// Replace with the real widget under test.
Widget compactTitle(String title, {Key? key}) => Text(
      title,
      key: key,
      maxLines: 1,
      overflow: TextOverflow.ellipsis,
      softWrap: false,
    );

Future<void> pumpTitle(
  WidgetTester tester,
  String title, {
  required double scale,
  double width = 160,
}) {
  return tester.pumpWidget(
    MaterialApp(
      home: MediaQuery(
        data: MediaQueryData(textScaler: TextScaler.linear(scale)),
        child: Scaffold(
          body: Center(
            child: SizedBox(
              width: width,
              child: compactTitle(title, key: const Key('title')),
            ),
          ),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  );
}

RenderParagraph paragraphOf(WidgetTester tester) => tester.renderObject(
      find.descendant(
        of: find.byKey(const Key('title')),
        matching: find.byType(RichText),
      ),
    );

void main() {
  const cases = <String, String>{
    'short': 'Inbox',
    'newline': 'Weekly\nsummary',
    'multiple newlines': 'Line one\n\nLine three\n',
    'long words': 'A considerably longer server title than fits',
    'long unbroken': 'Supercalifragilisticexpialidocious_ünïcödé_token',
  };

  for (final scale in [1.0, 1.5, 2.0]) {
    for (final entry in cases.entries) {
      testWidgets('title stays on one line: ${entry.key} @ ${scale}x',
          (tester) async {
        // Reference height for a single line at this scale.
        await pumpTitle(tester, 'X', scale: scale);
        final oneLineHeight =
            tester.getSize(find.byKey(const Key('title'))).height;

        await pumpTitle(tester, entry.value, scale: scale);

        expect(tester.takeException(), isNull);
        expect(
          tester.getSize(find.byKey(const Key('title'))).height,
          moreOrLessEquals(oneLineHeight, epsilon: 0.5),
          reason: 'Title rendered more than one line',
        );

        final paragraph = paragraphOf(tester);
        if (entry.key == 'short') {
          expect(paragraph.didExceedMaxLines, isFalse);
        } else if (entry.key.contains('newline') || entry.key.contains('long')) {
          // Newline copy and over-width copy must be truncated, not grown.
          expect(paragraph.didExceedMaxLines, isTrue);
        }
      });
    }
  }
}
```

**Why this catches the regression:** with the teammate's version (`softWrap: false`, no `maxLines`), the `newline` and `multiple newlines` cases fail the height assertion because the widget renders two or more lines. The `didExceedMaxLines` assertions also fail, because without `maxLines` there is no cap to exceed.

If you adopt newline normalization, add a case asserting that the displayed text is the flattened string, for example `find.text('Weekly summary')`. The height check stays as the guard against future regressions.

