I'd recommend not calling the payment API from `build()`. Flutter can call `build` many times for one cart state: parent rebuilds, theme or MediaQuery changes, hot reload, keyboard animations. Each call would be another payment request, so a customer could be **charged more than once**.

Instead, trigger the payment **once, on the transition** into "ready," and make the call idempotent.

## Recommended pattern

Keep `build` pure. React to the state change in a listener:

```dart
class CheckoutPage extends StatefulWidget {
  const CheckoutPage({super.key, required this.cart, required this.payments});
  final CartController cart;        // ChangeNotifier exposing `status`
  final PaymentService payments;

  @override
  State<CheckoutPage> createState() => _CheckoutPageState();
}

class _CheckoutPageState extends State<CheckoutPage> {
  CartStatus? _lastStatus;
  Future<PaymentResult>? _payment; // in-flight / completed guard

  @override
  void initState() {
    super.initState();
    _lastStatus = widget.cart.status;
    widget.cart.addListener(_onCartChanged);
    if (_lastStatus == CartStatus.ready) _startPayment();
  }

  @override
  void didUpdateWidget(CheckoutPage old) {
    super.didUpdateWidget(old);
    if (old.cart != widget.cart) {
      old.cart.removeListener(_onCartChanged);
      widget.cart.addListener(_onCartChanged);
    }
  }

  void _onCartChanged() {
    final status = widget.cart.status;
    final becameReady =
        _lastStatus != CartStatus.ready && status == CartStatus.ready;
    _lastStatus = status;
    if (becameReady) _startPayment();
  }

  void _startPayment() {
    if (_payment != null) return; // already paying / paid
    setState(() {
      _payment = widget.payments.charge(
        cartId: widget.cart.id,
        amount: widget.cart.total,
        // Server-side dedupe: same cart -> same key -> at most one charge.
        idempotencyKey: 'cart-${widget.cart.id}-${widget.cart.version}',
      );
    });
  }

  @override
  void dispose() {
    widget.cart.removeListener(_onCartChanged);
    super.dispose();
  }

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    // Pure: only *renders* payment state, never starts it.
    final payment = _payment;
    if (payment == null) return const Text('Preparing cart…');
    return FutureBuilder<PaymentResult>(
      future: payment, // stored Future, not created here
      builder: (context, snap) => switch (snap) {
        AsyncSnapshot(hasError: true) => Text('Payment failed: ${snap.error}'),
        AsyncSnapshot(hasData: true)  => Text('Paid: ${snap.data!.receiptId}'),
        _                             => const CircularProgressIndicator(),
      },
    );
  }
}
```

## If you use a state-management package

- **Riverpod:** use `ref.listen(cartProvider, (prev, next) { if (prev?.status != ready && next.status == ready) ref.read(paymentController.notifier).pay(next); });` inside `build`. `ref.listen` is designed for side effects and fires only on changes. Keep the in-flight guard inside `PaymentController`.
- **Bloc:** wrap the page in `BlocListener<CartBloc, CartState>` with `listenWhen: (p, n) => p.status != ready && n.status == ready`, and dispatch a `PayRequested` event to a payment bloc.

## Safeguards worth keeping regardless

1. **Transition check** (`prev != ready && next == ready`), not just `status == ready`.
2. **Client in-flight guard** so a second trigger is ignored while a request is pending.
3. **Idempotency key** sent to the payment backend. This is the real protection against duplicate charges from retries, restarts, or double taps.
4. **Explicit user consent.** Most payment flows should still charge only after the user taps "Pay." Then "cart ready" just enables the button. If the charge truly must be automatic, make sure that's intended product behavior.

If you tell me which state management you use (setState/ChangeNotifier, Riverpod, Bloc, Provider), I can tailor the snippet to your code.

