I'd steer away from calling the payment API from `build`. Flutter can call `build` many times for one logical state: theme changes, keyboard insets, parent rebuilds, hot reload, scrolling. Each call would fire another request, and a payment endpoint can charge the customer more than once.

The fix is to trigger the call on the **transition** to ready, from code that lives with the widget's lifecycle. That code should be idempotent per cart and ignore stale results. Here's a version that keeps your existing cart model and payment service unchanged:

```dart
class CheckoutPayment extends StatefulWidget {
  const CheckoutPayment({
    super.key,
    required this.cart,          // e.g. a ValueListenable<CartState> / ChangeNotifier
    required this.paymentService,
  });

  final ValueListenable<CartState> cart;
  final PaymentService paymentService;

  @override
  State<CheckoutPayment> createState() => _CheckoutPaymentState();
}

class _CheckoutPaymentState extends State<CheckoutPayment> {
  bool _wasReady = false;
  String? _chargedCartId;     // guards duplicate charges for the same cart
  int _requestToken = 0;      // drops results from superseded requests
  bool _submitting = false;
  Object? _error;

  @override
  void initState() {
    super.initState();
    widget.cart.addListener(_onCartChanged);
    // Defer so we never start side effects during the first build.
    WidgetsBinding.instance.addPostFrameCallback((_) => _onCartChanged());
  }

  @override
  void didUpdateWidget(covariant CheckoutPayment oldWidget) {
    super.didUpdateWidget(oldWidget);
    if (oldWidget.cart != widget.cart) {
      oldWidget.cart.removeListener(_onCartChanged);
      widget.cart.addListener(_onCartChanged);
      _wasReady = false;
      _onCartChanged();
    }
  }

  void _onCartChanged() {
    if (!mounted) return;
    final cart = widget.cart.value;
    final becameReady = cart.isReady && !_wasReady;
    _wasReady = cart.isReady;
    if (becameReady) _pay(cart);
  }

  Future<void> _pay(CartState cart) async {
    if (_submitting || _chargedCartId == cart.id) return;

    final token = ++_requestToken;
    setState(() {
      _submitting = true;
      _error = null;
    });

    try {
      await widget.paymentService.pay(
        cartId: cart.id,
        idempotencyKey: cart.id, // let the backend dedupe too, if it supports it
      );
      if (!mounted || token != _requestToken) return;
      setState(() => _chargedCartId = cart.id);
    } catch (e) {
      if (!mounted || token != _requestToken) return;
      setState(() => _error = e);
    } finally {
      if (mounted && token == _requestToken) {
        setState(() => _submitting = false);
      }
    }
  }

  @override
  void dispose() {
    widget.cart.removeListener(_onCartChanged);
    _requestToken++; // invalidate any in-flight result
    super.dispose();
  }

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    // Pure: renders state only, never starts work.
    if (_submitting) return const Center(child: CircularProgressIndicator());
    if (_error != null) {
      return _PaymentError(
        onRetry: () {
          _chargedCartId = null;
          _pay(widget.cart.value);
        },
      );
    }
    if (_chargedCartId != null) return const _PaymentSuccess();
    return const _WaitingForCart();
  }
}
```

**Why this shape**
- **Edge-triggered:** the call fires only on a not-ready → ready change, not on every rebuild.
- **Duplicate-safe:** the `_submitting` and `_chargedCartId` guards stop repeat calls on the client. The idempotency key lets the server reject repeats as well.
- **Stale-safe:** results that arrive after dispose, or after a newer request started, are dropped. That avoids `setState` on an unmounted widget and avoids showing the wrong outcome.
- **`build` stays pure**, so you can test each rendered state on its own.

**If you use a state-management package**, the same idea maps onto its listener:
- Riverpod: `ref.listen`
- Bloc: `BlocListener` with `listenWhen: (p, c) => !p.isReady && c.isReady`
- Provider/ChangeNotifier: a listener in the state object

Keep the guards either way. If you tell me which one you use, I'll adapt the code.

**A product question to confirm:** do you really want to charge as soon as the cart is ready? Most checkouts wait for an explicit "Pay" tap. If you want that, keep `_pay` and its guards, and call it from the button's `onPressed` instead of the listener. Disable the button while `_submitting` is true.

**Tests worth adding:**
- Forcing extra rebuilds while ready does not call `pay` again.
- Going ready → not ready → ready for the same cart charges once.
- Disposing mid-request causes no `setState` error.
- Retry after an error charges once.

