A literal `url.startsWith('https://help.example.com')` check is unsafe. It also matches `https://help.example.com.evil.com/...` and `https://help.example.com@evil.com/...`, so a redirect could land a phishing page inside your payment flow. Parse the URL and compare the scheme and host exactly instead.

Here's an implementation using `webview_flutter` and `url_launcher`:

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
import 'package:url_launcher/url_launcher.dart';
import 'package:webview_flutter/webview_flutter.dart';

const _trustedScheme = 'https';
const _trustedHost = 'help.example.com';

/// True only for https://help.example.com (default port), any path.
bool isTrustedHelpUrl(String url) {
  final uri = Uri.tryParse(url);
  if (uri == null) return false;
  return uri.scheme == _trustedScheme &&
      uri.host.toLowerCase() == _trustedHost &&
      !uri.hasPort &&          // reject https://help.example.com:8443
      uri.userInfo.isEmpty;    // reject https://user@help.example.com
}

class PaymentHelpPage extends StatefulWidget {
  const PaymentHelpPage({super.key});

  @override
  State<PaymentHelpPage> createState() => _PaymentHelpPageState();
}

class _PaymentHelpPageState extends State<PaymentHelpPage> {
  late final WebViewController _controller;

  @override
  void initState() {
    super.initState();
    _controller = WebViewController()
      ..setJavaScriptMode(JavaScriptMode.unrestricted) // only if the page needs JS
      ..setNavigationDelegate(
        NavigationDelegate(
          // Called for link taps, JS navigations and (on iOS / most Android
          // cases) server redirects, so redirects under the trusted origin
          // stay in-app and anything else is handed off.
          onNavigationRequest: (request) async {
            if (isTrustedHelpUrl(request.url)) {
              return NavigationDecision.navigate;
            }
            await _openExternally(request.url);
            return NavigationDecision.prevent;
          },
          onUrlChange: (change) {
            // Defense in depth: if a redirect slipped past the delegate
            // (some Android 3xx paths don't trigger it), leave the page.
            final url = change.url;
            if (url != null && !isTrustedHelpUrl(url)) {
              _openExternally(url);
              if (mounted) Navigator.of(context).maybePop();
            }
          },
        ),
      )
      ..loadRequest(Uri.parse('https://help.example.com/payments'));
  }

  Future<void> _openExternally(String url) async {
    final uri = Uri.tryParse(url);
    if (uri == null) return;
    // Only hand off schemes you expect; drop javascript:, file:, intent:, etc.
    const allowed = {'https', 'http', 'mailto', 'tel'};
    if (!allowed.contains(uri.scheme)) return;
    await launchUrl(uri, mode: LaunchMode.externalApplication);
  }

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      appBar: AppBar(title: const Text('Payment help')),
      body: WebViewWidget(controller: _controller),
    );
  }
}
```

How it meets your three requirements:

- **Stays in the WebView:** any URL whose scheme is `https` and whose host is exactly `help.example.com` loads in-app, whatever the path.
- **Redirects:** redirects that stay on that origin are allowed. A redirect to anywhere else is blocked and opened externally. `onUrlChange` acts as a backstop in case the platform doesn't send a redirect through `onNavigationRequest`.
- **Everything else:** other URLs open in the system browser or app via `launchUrl`. Only an allowlisted set of schemes is opened.

Some decisions to confirm:

1. **Subdomains:** if `docs.help.example.com` should also be trusted, change the host check to `host == _trustedHost || host.endsWith('.$_trustedHost')`. Keep the leading dot.
2. **Path scoping:** if you meant a narrower prefix such as `https://help.example.com/payments`, also check `uri.path == '/payments' || uri.path.startsWith('/payments/')`.
3. **Platform setup:** on Android 11+, add `<queries>` entries for `https`, `mailto` and `tel` intents to the manifest. On iOS, add `LSApplicationQueriesSchemes` if you call `canLaunchUrl`.
4. **Tests:** add unit tests for `isTrustedHelpUrl`. Cover lookalike hosts (`help.example.com.evil.com`), userinfo tricks (`help.example.com@evil.com`), `http://`, explicit ports, and uppercase hosts.

