I'll keep the help page in the WebView and open other links externally, but with one change: **I won't use a literal `startsWith('https://help.example.com')` check.** That string prefix also matches attacker-controlled URLs:

| URL | `startsWith` result | Actual host |
|---|---|---|
| `https://help.example.com.evil.net/pay` | match | `help.example.com.evil.net` |
| `https://help.example.com@evil.net/` | match | `evil.net` (the prefix is just user-info) |
| `https://help.example.com:8443/` | match | different origin (other port) |
| `https://help.example.community/` | match | different domain |

On a payment page, any of these would load phishing content inside your app's chrome. The rule below matches what "starts with `https://help.example.com`" is meant to express: the exact origin.

## Policy

- **Stay in the WebView:** the scheme is `https`, the host is exactly `help.example.com` (case-insensitive, no trailing dot, no punycode lookalikes), the port is 443, there is no user-info, and the path can be anything. Every redirect hop goes through the same check, so a redirect that leaves the origin also leaves the WebView.
- **Open externally:** a main-frame navigation anywhere else, using `https`, `http`, `mailto`, or `tel`. These open in the system browser or the matching app.
- **Block, with a recoverable state:** URLs that can't be parsed; other schemes such as `javascript:`, `file:`, `intent:`, `data:`, or custom app schemes; and subframe navigations to other origins. An iframe should never be able to launch your external browser. If you need a specific scheme such as a banking app's, add it to the allowlist explicitly.

Two other points:

- **Payment outcomes:** reaching a URL like `/payment/success` proves nothing. Confirm any payment outcome through your payment backend, not through WebView navigation.
- **Assumptions:** this is written against `webview_flutter` 4.x `NavigationDelegate` and an injected `url_launcher`-style launcher. If your screen uses a different package, the policy and tests stay the same and only the wiring changes. JavaScript stays disabled unless the help page actually needs it.

## `lib/help/help_navigation_policy.dart`

```dart
enum NavigationOutcome { inWebView, external, blocked }

class HelpNavigationPolicy {
  const HelpNavigationPolicy();

  static const _host = 'help.example.com';
  static const _externalSchemes = {'https', 'http', 'mailto', 'tel'};

  bool isHelpOrigin(Uri uri) =>
      uri.scheme == 'https' &&
      uri.host == _host && // Uri normalizes host case; trailing dot / punycode won't match
      uri.port == 443 && // explicit :443 is fine, any other port is not
      uri.userInfo.isEmpty;

  NavigationOutcome decide(String rawUrl, {required bool isMainFrame}) {
    final uri = Uri.tryParse(rawUrl);
    if (uri == null || !uri.hasScheme) return NavigationOutcome.blocked;
    if (isHelpOrigin(uri)) return NavigationOutcome.inWebView;
    if (!isMainFrame) return NavigationOutcome.blocked;
    final scheme = uri.scheme.toLowerCase();
    if (!_externalSchemes.contains(scheme)) return NavigationOutcome.blocked;
    if ((scheme == 'https' || scheme == 'http') && uri.host.isEmpty) {
      return NavigationOutcome.blocked;
    }
    return NavigationOutcome.external;
  }
}
```

## `lib/help/help_webview_coordinator.dart`

This file holds the navigation, error, and back logic in plain Dart. The launcher and history are passed in, so tests don't need a real WebView.

```dart
import 'help_navigation_policy.dart';

typedef ExternalLauncher = Future<bool> Function(Uri uri);

abstract class WebHistory {
  Future<bool> canGoBack();
  Future<void> goBack();
}

sealed class HelpPageState { const HelpPageState(); }
class Loading extends HelpPageState { const Loading(); }
class Loaded extends HelpPageState { const Loaded(); }
class Blocked extends HelpPageState { const Blocked(this.url); final String url; }
class LoadFailed extends HelpPageState { const LoadFailed(this.reason); final String reason; }
class ExternalLaunchFailed extends HelpPageState { const ExternalLaunchFailed(this.url); final String url; }

enum BackResult { wentBackInWebView, exitScreen }

class HelpWebViewCoordinator {
  HelpWebViewCoordinator({
    required this.launcher,
    required this.onState,
    this.policy = const HelpNavigationPolicy(),
  });

  final ExternalLauncher launcher;
  final void Function(HelpPageState) onState;
  final HelpNavigationPolicy policy;
  bool _disposed = false;

  /// Returns true if the WebView may navigate.
  Future<bool> onNavigationRequest(String url, {required bool isMainFrame}) async {
    switch (policy.decide(url, isMainFrame: isMainFrame)) {
      case NavigationOutcome.inWebView:
        return true;
      case NavigationOutcome.external:
        final ok = await _safeLaunch(Uri.parse(url));
        if (!ok) _emit(ExternalLaunchFailed(url));
        return false;
      case NavigationOutcome.blocked:
        if (isMainFrame) _emit(Blocked(url));
        return false;
    }
  }

  void onMainFrameError(String reason) => _emit(LoadFailed(reason));
  void onHttpError(int statusCode, {required bool isMainFrame}) {
    if (isMainFrame && statusCode >= 400) _emit(LoadFailed('HTTP $statusCode'));
  }
  void onPageFinished() => _emit(const Loaded());

  Future<BackResult> handleBack(WebHistory history) async {
    if (await history.canGoBack()) {
      await history.goBack();
      return BackResult.wentBackInWebView;
    }
    return BackResult.exitScreen;
  }

  void dispose() => _disposed = true;

  Future<bool> _safeLaunch(Uri uri) async {
    try {
      return await launcher(uri);
    } catch (_) {
      return false;
    }
  }

  void _emit(HelpPageState s) {
    if (!_disposed) onState(s); // late callbacks after dispose are dropped
  }
}
```

## Wiring in the screen

```dart
_coordinator = HelpWebViewCoordinator(
  launcher: (uri) => launchUrl(uri, mode: LaunchMode.externalApplication),
  onState: (s) { if (mounted) setState(() => _state = s); },
);

_controller = WebViewController()
  ..setJavaScriptMode(JavaScriptMode.disabled) // enable only if the help page requires it
  ..setNavigationDelegate(NavigationDelegate(
    onNavigationRequest: (req) async =>
        await _coordinator.onNavigationRequest(req.url, isMainFrame: req.isMainFrame)
            ? NavigationDecision.navigate
            : NavigationDecision.prevent,
    onPageFinished: (_) => _coordinator.onPageFinished(),
    onWebResourceError: (e) {
      if (e.isForMainFrame ?? true) _coordinator.onMainFrameError(e.description);
    },
    onHttpError: (e) => _coordinator.onHttpError(
      e.response?.statusCode ?? 0,
      isMainFrame: true, // narrow by request URL if your version exposes it
    ),
    // If your webview_flutter version exposes onSslAuthError, always cancel(); never proceed().
  ))
  ..loadRequest(Uri.parse('https://help.example.com/payments'));

@override
void dispose() {
  _coordinator.dispose();
  super.dispose();
}
```

For back handling, wrap the screen in `PopScope(canPop: false, onPopInvokedWithResult: ...)`. Call `_coordinator.handleBack(...)` with an adapter over `_controller.canGoBack()` and `goBack()`, and pop the route when the result is `exitScreen`.

## `test/help/help_navigation_policy_test.dart`

```dart
void main() {
  const policy = HelpNavigationPolicy();
  NavigationOutcome main(String u) => policy.decide(u, isMainFrame: true);

  group('stays in WebView', () {
    for (final u in [
      'https://help.example.com',
      'https://help.example.com/',
      'https://help.example.com/payments/refunds?id=1#faq',
      'https://HELP.Example.COM/payments',
      'https://help.example.com:443/payments',
    ]) {
      test(u, () => expect(main(u), NavigationOutcome.inWebView));
    }
  });

  group('near-matches never stay in WebView', () {
    for (final u in [
      'https://help.example.com.evil.net/pay',
      'https://help.example.com@evil.net/',
      'https://user:pw@help.example.com/',
      'https://help.example.community/',
      'https://help.example.com:8443/',
      'http://help.example.com/',
      'https://help.example.com./',
      'https://xn--hlp-example-xyz.com/',
    ]) {
      test(u, () => expect(main(u), isNot(NavigationOutcome.inWebView)));
    }
  });

  test('ordinary external links open externally', () {
    expect(main('https://www.example.com/terms'), NavigationOutcome.external);
    expect(main('mailto:support@example.com'), NavigationOutcome.external);
  });

  test('dangerous or unknown schemes and malformed URLs are blocked', () {
    for (final u in [
      'javascript:alert(1)',
      'file:///etc/passwd',
      'intent://x#Intent;end',
      'data:text/html,hi',
      'myapp://pay/confirm',
      'http://[::1',
      '',
      'https://',
    ]) {
      expect(main(u), NavigationOutcome.blocked, reason: u);
    }
  });

  test('cross-origin subframes are blocked, never launched', () {
    expect(policy.decide('https://ads.evil.net/', isMainFrame: false),
        NavigationOutcome.blocked);
    expect(policy.decide('https://help.example.com/widget', isMainFrame: false),
        NavigationOutcome.inWebView);
  });

  test('redirect chain is checked hop by hop', () {
    final hops = [
      'https://help.example.com/pay',
      'https://help.example.com/pay/step2',
      'https://help.example.com.evil.net/pay/step3', // leaves origin mid-chain
    ];
    expect(hops.map(main).toList(), [
      NavigationOutcome.inWebView,
      NavigationOutcome.inWebView,
      NavigationOutcome.external,
    ]);
  });

  test('popup / new-window URL uses the same policy', () {
    expect(main('https://partner.example.org/popup'), NavigationOutcome.external);
    expect(main('https://help.example.com/popup'), NavigationOutcome.inWebView);
  });
}
```

## `test/help/help_webview_coordinator_test.dart`

```dart
class FakeHistory implements WebHistory {
  FakeHistory(this.depth);
  int depth;
  @override Future<bool> canGoBack() async => depth > 0;
  @override Future<void> goBack() async => depth--;
}

void main() {
  late List<HelpPageState> states;
  late List<Uri> launched;
  HelpWebViewCoordinator make({bool launchOk = true, bool throws = false}) =>
      HelpWebViewCoordinator(
        launcher: (u) async {
          if (throws) throw Exception('no handler');
          launched.add(u);
          return launchOk;
        },
        onState: states.add,
      );
  setUp(() { states = []; launched = []; });

  test('external link is launched and WebView navigation prevented', () async {
    final c = make();
    expect(await c.onNavigationRequest('https://www.example.com/', isMainFrame: true), isFalse);
    expect(launched.single.host, 'www.example.com');
  });

  test('failed or throwing external launch yields recoverable state', () async {
    for (final c in [make(launchOk: false), make(throws: true)]) {
      await c.onNavigationRequest('tel:+15551234', isMainFrame: true);
    }
    expect(states, everyElement(isA<ExternalLaunchFailed>()));
  });

  test('unparsable URL yields Blocked, nothing launched', () async {
    await make().onNavigationRequest('http://[::1', isMainFrame: true);
    expect(states.single, isA<Blocked>());
    expect(launched, isEmpty);
  });

  test('main-frame and HTTP errors yield LoadFailed', () {
    final c = make()
      ..onMainFrameError('net::ERR_INTERNET_DISCONNECTED')
      ..onHttpError(503, isMainFrame: true);
    expect(states, [isA<LoadFailed>(), isA<LoadFailed>()]);
  });

  test('back: web history first, then exit; same after external handoff', () async {
    final c = make();
    final h = FakeHistory(1);
    await c.onNavigationRequest('https://www.example.com/', isMainFrame: true); // handoff
    expect(await c.handleBack(h), BackResult.wentBackInWebView);
    expect(await c.handleBack(h), BackResult.exitScreen);
  });

  test('callbacks after dispose do not mutate state', () {
    final c = make()..dispose();
    c.onMainFrameError('late');
    expect(states, isEmpty);
  });
}
```

## Still needs a real device

Widget and unit tests prove the policy and the state ownership. They do not prove how the system WebView behaves. On each supported platform (Android System WebView and iOS WKWebView), check these against staging help content:

- server 30x redirects within the origin and off it
- `target=_blank` and `window.open` behavior (record it if the package doesn't surface them)
- `tel:` and `mailto:` handoff, including with no handler installed
- offline and HTTP 5xx responses
- a TLS error, which must not proceed
- back and forward navigation
- the app being killed and the WebView process recreated

Record the package version, OS, and WebView engine version alongside the results.

If the help page links to a hosted checkout or uses a non-HTTPS app scheme for payment, tell me which domains or schemes are involved. I'll add them as explicit policy entries rather than widening the host rule.
